      "id": "event-dot-com-bubble-bursts-2000",
      "year": 2000,
      "title": "Dot-com bubble bursts",
      "titleZh": "互联网泡沫破裂",
      "summary": "The collapse of many internet stocks in 2000 exposed the gap between web speculation and durable business models, but it did not end the internet economy.",
      "summaryZh": "2000 年许多互联网股票崩盘，暴露出网络投机与可持续商业模式之间的差距，但它并没有终结互联网经济。",
      "eventIntro": "In 2000, the speculative boom around internet companies broke sharply, especially on U.S. technology markets. Companies that had been valued on traffic, promise, and speed rather than profits suddenly faced capital scarcity, layoffs, and collapse.",
      "eventIntroZh": "2000 年，围绕互联网公司的投机热潮急剧破裂，尤其体现在美国科技市场上。许多此前依靠流量、愿景和速度而非利润获得高估值的公司，突然面对资本枯竭、裁员和倒闭。",
      "whyMatters": "The crash separated web infrastructure from web hype. It punished fragile firms, but it also left behind fiber networks, data centers, user habits, engineering talent, and surviving platforms that later powered search, e-commerce, cloud services, and social media.",
      "whyMattersZh": "这次崩盘把网络基础设施和网络泡沫区分开来。它惩罚了脆弱企业，但留下了光纤网络、数据中心、用户习惯、工程人才和幸存平台，后来支撑搜索、电商、云服务和社交媒体。",
      "phaseRelation": "This event belongs to the digital and platform economy phase because it marks the painful transition from speculative internet enthusiasm to a more disciplined platform capitalism.",
      "phaseRelationZh": "它属于“数字与平台经济”阶段，因为它标志着互联网狂热从投机性兴奋走向更严厉的平台资本主义纪律。",
      "connectionHint": "It connects to Google AdWords, enterprise Java, broadband platforms, and later cloud computing, because post-crash internet winners had to prove revenue, scale, and infrastructure discipline.",
